{"name":"Shell","hex":"#dcc5bc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Shell","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A diminutive of the female given names Michelle and Shelly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Shell"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shell (structure)","blocks":[{"text":"A shell is a three-dimensional solid structural element whose thickness is very small compared to its other dimensions. It is characterized in structural terms by mid-plane stress which is both coplanar and normal to the surface. A shell can be derived from a plate in two steps: by initially forming the middle surface as a singly or doubly curved surface, then by applying loads which are coplanar to the plate's plane thus generating significant stresses.\nMaterials range from concrete to fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/08terminal5.jpg/330px-08terminal5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shell_(structure)","wikidata":"Q2230313"},{"title":"Concrete shell","blocks":[{"text":"A concrete shell, also commonly called thin shell concrete structure, is a structure composed of a relatively thin shell of concrete, usually with no interior columns or exterior buttresses. The shells are most commonly monolithic domes, but may also take the form of hyperbolic paraboloids, ellipsoids, cylindrical sections, or some combination thereof. The first concrete shell dates back to the 2nd century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/%22l_%27oceanographic_%22_a_valence_-_panoramio.jpg/330px-%22l_%27oceanographic_%22_a_valence_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concrete_shell","wikidata":"Q3737546"},{"title":"Shell (projectile)","blocks":[{"text":"A shell, in a modern military context, is a projectile whose payload contains an explosive, incendiary, or other chemical filling. Originally it was called a bombshell, but \"shell\" has come to be unambiguous in a military context. A shell can hold a tracer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/WWI_shells.JPG/330px-WWI_shells.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shell_(projectile)","wikidata":"Q318028"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/British_Museum_Great_Court_roof.jpg/500px-British_Museum_Great_Court_roof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/British_Museum_Great_Court_roof.jpg/500px-British_Museum_Great_Court_roof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Great Court, with a lattice thin-shell roof by Buro Happold with Norman Foster, British Museum, London","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABritish_Museum_Great_Court_roof.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg/500px-Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg/1280px-Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg/500px-Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The dome of the Pantheon, Rome, the oldest known concrete shell","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARome_(Italy%2C_October_2019)_-_275_(50589571796).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Royan_Central_Market.jpg/500px-Royan_Central_Market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Royan_Central_Market.jpg/1280px-Royan_Central_Market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Royan_Central_Market.jpg/500px-Royan_Central_Market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Royan Central Market.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoyan_Central_Market.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg/500px-W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg/500px-W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"US scientists with a full-scale cut-away model of the W48 155 millimeter nuclear artillery shell, a very small tactical nuclear weapon with an explosive yield equivalent to 72 tons of TNT (0.072 kiloton). It could be fired from any standard 155 mm (6.1 inch) howitzer (e.g., the M114 or M198).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AW48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg/500px-USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg/1280px-USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb